## 1. Introduction
Antibiotics, a class of man–made chemicals, have aroused increasing attention recently since these components are nonbiodegradable and can cause adverse influence towards the ecosystem [1]. Many studies have found that their existence in surface water (around $54\sim260~\mathrm{\ng/L)}$ have potential acute and chronic toxicities towards human beings and can also spread antibiotic resistance genes [2–6]. Thus, exploring the effective removal techniques in complicated water matrix is meaningful [7]. Ciprofloxacin (CIP; $\mathsf{C}_{17}\mathsf{H}_{18}\mathsf{O}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{3}\mathsf{F})$ is one of the most commonly used pharmaceuticals and personal care products (PPCPs) with high antibacterial activity which is largely prescribed for digestive infections and respiratory treatment [8–11]. Adsorption is one of the most feasible technologies because of its easy operation and cost effectiveness, however, several challenges exist due to their complicated competitors in water [12]. For example, secondary clarifier effluents usually include certain amounts of natural organic matter (NOM) $\left(6{-}10~\mathrm{mg/L}\right)$ , inorganic ions $\mathrm{\langle10-110~mg/L\rangle}$ and trace amounts of antibiotics (e.g., ng– $\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{L}\uplambda$ [13]. Previous studies confirmed that the presence of humic acid in natural water decreased the PPCPs uptake capacities over nanomaterials by competing the binding sites onto the surface [14–15]. Thus, designing a novel material that can increase adsorption selectivity while achieve a high uptake capacity at the same time is of highly demand.
Molecular imprinting technology (MIT), is one of the smart strategies for constructing materials with tunable recognition sites which can selectively bind target compounds. Molecular imprinting Polymers (MIPs) are synthetic polymers with special pores tailored for target compound, which are prepared by copolymerization of a complex formed by a cross–linking component and a functional monomer together with a molecular template [16,17]. Usually the selected monomer links to the imprint compounds by H–bonding/van der Waals, electrostatic interactions, covalent bond or metal–linkers coordination [17]. Subsequent elimination of the imprinting template can leave a specific cavity with specific shapes and functional groups that can be complementary to the template molecule. However, conventional molecular imprinting polymers have many obstacles such as uneven loading of binding sites and slow mass transfer efficiency [18]. At present, the carrier materials in surface MIT mainly include $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles [19], graphene [20], titanium dioxide [21], silica nanoparticles [22], carbon nanotubes [23] etc. Previous studies also show that appropriate functional monomer selection is crucial. On the other hand, commonly functional monomers usually include acrylic acid (AA), 4–vinylpyridine (4–VP) and 3–aminopro pyltriethoxysilane (APTES) [17,24].
MOFs are a type of microporous/mesoporous crystal nanomaterial formed by the coordination of metal clusters and connected linkers [25]. It contains the advantages of ultrahigh surface area and adjustable pore size, making them an ideal material for gas adsorption [26], drug delivery [27], chemical separation [28], catalysis [29] and sensing [30]. It also arouses great attention in the field of pollutants elimination recently [31–32], but the selective removal in the real environment with complicated constituents is still challenging and need further studies. Currently, MOFs have been applied as carrier materials for surface molecularly imprinted materials including $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8$ [33], MIL–100(Fe) [34] and $\ N{\mathrm{H}}_{2^{-}}$
MIL–101(Cr) [35]. However, the detailed explanation of the mechanism is limited.
Therefore, in this study, a MOF–based (MIL–100(Fe)) imprinted molecule has been designed and synthesized successfully with a microwaved–assistance method. APTES and TEOS have been selected to form the core–shell imprinting layer outside. CIP was selected as the model target molecule. The core–shell structure has been characterized in detail. Then, the selective adsorption and photo-Fenton degradation processes were evaluated systematically including competitive adsorption in simulated wastewater and real wastewater. Furthermore, the removal mechanism has been proposed to give a comprehensive understanding of this removal process. This study offers a useful guidance for MOF–directed material design and applications in aqueous phase.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Chemicals
1,3,5-Benzenetricarboxylic (BTC, CAS: 554–95-0, $298\%$ ), Tetraethylorthosilicate (TEOS, $98\%$ , CAS: 78–10-4, MW 208.33, ${\mathsf{C}}_{8}-$ $\mathrm{H}_{20}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{Si}^{\cdot}$ ), (3–Aminopropyl) triethoxysilane (APTES, $98\%$ , CAS:919– 30-2, MW 221.37 $\mathrm{C_{9}H_{23}N O_{3}S i}$ , humic acid (HA) and Ciprofloxacin (CIP, $99\%$ , $\mathsf{C}_{17}\mathrm{H}_{18}\mathrm{FN}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ , MW 331.34) were purchased from Aladdin Chemicals. The iron chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $99\%$ ), ethanol $(99\%)$ , methanol $(99\%)$ , acetic acid $(99\%)$ and ethylene glycol $(99\%)$ were reagent grade from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). All solutions were prepared with ultrapure water (resistivity $>18~\mathrm{M}\Omega$ prepared with a Mill-Q system (Millipore, Billerica, USA).
## 2.2. Synthesis of target materials
$\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles were synthesized by a solvothermal method as reported [36]. Core–shell magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-100$ (Fe) was synthesized by a microwave irradiation method as reported in literature [37]. These specific synthesized procedures are discribed in SI. The core–shell magnetic $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L-100(F e}$ ) ${\ @\mathrm{MIP}}$ microparticles with three layers were synthesized by sol– gel method [33,38]. First, CIP $(0.354~\mathrm{g})$ and (3–Aminopropyl) triethoxysilane) $(0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ ) were dispersed in ethanol with addition of obtained activated $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})$ , then stirred for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Next, TEOS and HAc were added to above solution and stirred for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . After condensing at $60~^{\circ}C$ for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ , the resultant material was washed several times. The material was washed alternately with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and methanol/acetic acid and then washed by methanol until pH was close to neutral. In order to remove the template molecule CIP, the product was extracted by $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ for 4 days. Finally, the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L-100(F e)@M I P}$ was dried in vacuum at $100^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}.$ This specific synthesized procedures are discribed in SI.
## 2.3. Characterization
X–ray powder diffraction (XRD, Rigaku Mini FlEX 600) was used to determine the crystal structures of samples. The chemical structures of samples were tested by Fourier Transform infrared spectroscopy (FT–IR, Bruker Alpha). The surface area and ore size distribution were tested by Quanta chrome ASiQMVH002–5. X–ray photoelectron spectroscopy and TG were conducted with JSM–6400 spectrometer and Shimadzu DTG–60 thermal analyzer under nitrogen atmosphere. Zeta potential was measured using the Malvern Zetasizer Nano ZS90. Detailed operated procedures are described in detail in SI.
## 2.4. Adsorption experiment
Adsorption studies were carried out in magnetically stirred glass vessels at room temperature. Most experiments are repeated twice. The concentrations of pharmaceuticals were all measured by high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC, 1260, Agilent) or liquid chromatograph–mass spectrometer (LC–MS, Agilent 1290 Infinity II).
## 2.4.1. Adsorption kinetics
$10\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})@\mathrm{MIP}.$ , or $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ - $0_{4}@\mathsf{M I L}-100(\mathsf{F e})@\mathsf{N I P}$ were added to a bottle containing CIP aqueous solution $40~\mathrm{mL}$ , 50 ppm). At regular intervals, concentrations of CIP were determined.
## 2.4.2. Adsorption isotherms
A series of adsorbents were added to the CIP aqueous solution at different initial concentrations $(\mathsf C_{0}\colon10,20,30,40,50\mathrm{ppm})$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ to ensure adsorption balance. The concentration of the adsorbents was $0.25~\mathrm{g/L}$ . The supernatant was collected, followed by HPLC analysis of the residual CIP concentration.
## 2.4.3. Selective sorption study
Three PPCPs (Sulfadimethoxine (SMT), Ibuprofen (IBU) and Carbamazepine (CBZ)) are compared with Ciprofloxacin (CIP) to evaluate the removal selectivity. The chemical structures and properties of the four pharmaceuticals are listed in Table 1. Adsorbent was added to a single PPCPs solution $(50\ \mathrm{ppm})$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to ensure adsorption balance. The concentration of the adsorbents was $0.25~\mathrm{g/L}$ . The supernatant was collected, followed by HPLC analysis of the residual PPCPs concentration. Imprinting factor $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ and selectivity factor $\upbeta$ were applied as indicators to describe the selectivity of the adsorbent [39].
