## 2.4.4. Effect of pH
Adsorbents were added to CIP solution $(50\mathrm{ppm})$ at different pH for $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ to ensure adsorption balance. The concentration of the adsorbents was $0.25~\mathrm{g/L}$ . The solution pH was adjusted to 3, 5, 7, 9, and 11 with 1 mol L–1 HCl or NaOH.
## 2.4.5. Effect of inorganic ions and humic acid $(H A)$
50 ppm CIP solution containing NaCl, ${\mathsf{N a N O}}_{3}$ , ${\tt N a}_{2}S{\tt O}_{4}$ , KCl, $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ $(50\mathrm{mmol/L})$ and HA $(200\ \mathsf{p p m})$ was prepared, respectively. Adsorbents were added to the CIP solution $50\mathrm{ppm})$ with different inorganic ions with high concentrations. The concentration of the adsorbents was $0.25~\mathrm{g/L}$ .
## 2.4.6. Experiments in real wastewater
## 2.4.7. Regeneration performance
First, $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L-100(F e)@M I P}$ was dispersed in the CIP $\left(50\mathrm{\mg}/\mathrm{\L}\right)$ solution. After reaching the adsorption equilibrium, the MIP was separated using a magnet, and then washed by a mixed solution of methanol/acetic acid $(90{:}10,\upnu/\upnu)$ for 2 times. Then, the product was washed to neutral with anhydrous methanol and dried under vacuum. The same procedure was repeated to evaluate the reusability of the material. The residual CIP concentrations were collected and then analyzed by HPLC.
## 2.5. PPCPs analysis
The concentration of pharmaceuticals was determined by high– performance liquid chromatography (HPLC). A Zorbax XDB–C18 column $(4.6\ \mathrm{mm}\times250\ \mathrm{mm},5\ \upmu\mathrm{m})$ was used for chromatographic analysis. Prior to injection, all solutions were filtered through a $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ membrane to remove any solid particles, the column temperature was set at $30^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ the injection volume was $20\upmu\mathrm{L}$ . The flow rate of mobile phase was $1\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{min}$ . Detailed detection wavelength (nm) and mobile phase ratios are as follows Table 1.
## 2.6. Degradation experiment
Degradation study was carried out in photochemical reactor (Zhongjiao Jinyuan, CEL–LB70) at room temperature. First, adsorbents were added to the CIP solution $(50\ \mathrm{ppm})$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to ensure adsorption balance. The volume was $10~\mathrm{mL}$ After that, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was added to the saturated adsorption solution and the visible light was turned on at the same time. At regular intervals, CIP concentrations were determined. The concentration of the adsorbents was $0.25~\mathrm{g/L}$ . The optical power density was about $100\mathrm{\mW}/\mathrm{cm}2$ . The concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was $40~\mathrm{mM}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Fabrication of materials
The preparation procedure of the MIP is described in Fig. 1. In this work, the synthesis of the MIP material was specially addressed to extract CIP molecular in complicated water environment. Firstly, we successfully synthesized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles as the precursor via solvothermal method which can serve as the crystal seed to heterogeneously graft MOFs shell in situ. This approach consists of three steps including (a) the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ magnetic core support the organic building blocks $(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C})$ grew. It is reasonable to grow Fe–based MOFs onto the surface of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ microspheres since the good complexation ability between Fe (III) in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ and $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ . In comparison with the traditional solvothermal methods to synthesize MOFs, the microwave technology can decrease the reaction time dramatically from $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to $30\mathrm{min}$ with more MOFs yield. (b) Then the imprinted polymer was synthesized applying a sol–gel process. We used the resulting spheres above as the matrix, antibiotic as the template molecule, 3–Aminopropyl) triethoxysilane (APTES) as functional monomer to create specific binding site and TEOS as the crosslinker. The simultaneous hydrolysis of functional precursor by replacing $-C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}0$ by $-\mathsf{O H}$ in aqueous phase and the condensation of the hydrolyzed precursors will initiate the gel–sol process to form three–dimensional polymer network (Si–O–Si). The composite with multiple layers was obtained by the strong H–bonding and electrostatic attraction between hydroxyl portion (e.g. carboxylic group) in CIP and amino group $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ of APTES, then attached to the surface of porous MOFs. In this process, HAC was added as a reaction catalyst to facilitate the hydrolysis of TEOS to form the 3D polymer network. After rection for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ , the three–layered MIPs material was generated probably by the crosslinking process between the abundant hydrogen–binding binding sites (e.g. –COOH groups) on surface of MOFs and target compound. (c) Then the CIP molecules were removed with HAc: methanol mixture $(1/9,\upnu;\upnu)$ . Then the novel core–shell surface molecularly imprinted polymer with the memory of the template compound in shape and size that facilitate the specific extraction of CIP was synthesized that consisted of a magnetic core and a MIL–100(Fe) shell.
## 3.2. Characterization of as–synthesized materials
The three–layered structure of the MIP was characterized by TEM (Fig. 2). A spherical shape can be observed from the core–shell nanomaterials. A layer of MOFs crystals can be clearly found on the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L-100(F e}$ ). This surface shell is favorable for the generation of MIP layers which can facilitate PPCPs adsorption into the abundant surface areas. Moreover, a layer of MOFs shell with the thickness of around $50{-}100\mathrm{nm}$ surround the magnetic core inside. This founding indicates that the MOF layer is rough. On the surface many nano sized MOFs crystals were introduced. A good performance of high antibiotic uptake could beneficial to the high surface area of the surface shell of MOFs layers. Fig. 2(c) shows that the surface of the MOFs used to coat the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanospheres is covered with a layer with a thickness of about $30~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , indicating that the MIP successfully grew onto the surface of the support.
As shown in Fig. 2 (d). the distribution of elements in this core– shell structure was observed by high–angle annular dark–field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF–STEM). This offers the elemental composition and crystal information at atomic scale. $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L-100(F e)@M I P}$ consists of the elements of Fe, C, O and Si. Fig. 2 (e–h) shows that the iron is concentrated in the core part, while the other elements including the Si, O and C are evenly separated surround the core part. The Si signal demonstrated that the imprinted S–contained polymers evenly distributed outside.
As shown in Fig. 3 (a), the powder–XRD of as–synthesized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ and MIL–100 is of crystallinity during the synthesized process. The PXRD patterns of the three materials are consistent with the standard card (JCPDS No. 19–0629). For example, the series of diffraction peaks of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ appear at $2\time20=30^{\circ}$ , $35.3^{\circ}$ , 43, $56.9^{\circ}$ and $62.4^{\circ}$ correspond to the (220), (311), (400), (511) and (440) planes repetitively. Several typical characteristic diffraction peaks of MIL–100(Fe) appear at $2\uptheta=2.0^{\circ}$ , $3.3^{\circ}$ , $3.9^{\circ}$ and $11.0^{\circ}$ correspond to the (111), (022), (113), (428) planes of MIL–100 (CCDC number: 640536). It is noted that a new characteristic diffraction peaks at $18^{\circ}{\sim}30^{\circ}\mathrm{of}$ the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})@\mathrm{MIP}$ nanoparticles were observed, indicating the presence of imprinted layer of the composite, which demonstrated that the imprinted layer successfully grafted the surface of MOF. This result is in good agreement with the above description.
To further confirm the structure, FT–IR spectra of three materials have been conducted. The stretching vibrations of Fe–O–Fe bonds in the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ can be observed at around $569\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ and correspond to the previous study (Fig. 3 (b)) [40]. The peaks at $1634~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ and $1382~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are characteristic signals of $\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{V a s}}(\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{O})$ , and $\mathsf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{v s}}(\mathsf{C}-0)$ respectively, which indicates the formation of MOF supported by carboxyl group [37]. The bands present at $1060\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be attributed to stretching vibrations of Si–O bonds in the APTES and TEOS structure [33], which proves the presence of the MIP layer on the surface of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L-100(F}$ e).
To further investigate the configuration, XPS was applied. In Fig. 3 (c), the signals of 711.1 eV, 532.3 eV and $285.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be attributed to ${\sf F e}_{2\mathrm{p}}$ , $0_{1s}$ and ${\mathsf C}_{1s},$ respectively [37]. Additionally, the three new peaks of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L-100(F e)@M I P}$ observed near 401.3 eV, 154.1 eV and $103.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be attributed to N 1 s, Si 2 s and $\mathsf{S i~}2\mathsf{p}$ respectively [33], which demonstrates that TEOS and APTES were introduced into the surface of the three–layered nanoparticles successfully.
In addition, the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was employed In Fig. 3 (d). The $4.1\%$ slight mass loss of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ is attributed to the removal of water molecules on the surface of the material. The thermogravimetric curve of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L-100}$ (Fe) is consistent with that reported in the literature. Mass loss reaches a plateau (weight $-40.1\%)$ before $500^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ is attributed to the decomposition of the framework in MOF [41]. In comparison, the TGA curve of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})@\mathrm{MIP}$ reach a plateau (weight – $21.1\%$ at between 500 and $600^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ observed in Fig. 3 (d). This finding further confirms the successful growth of the robust imprinted layer outside. The zeta potential value of the material is below zero (negatively charged) at $\mathrm{pH}>3.6$ , which is consistent with the previous report [40,42]. After growing a layer of imprinted shell, the surface turned to be positively charged probably because $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ group on the surface of the imprinted layers changed to $\left.-\mathsf{N H}_{3}^{+}\right.$ in the protonation process [43].
To further describe the porous structure of the as–synthesized nanomaterials, nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms of above materials were conducted. In comparison, As shown in Fig. 4 (a), MOF support offers abundant binding sites thanks to the large BET surface area of $1190~\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $819\ {\mathrm{cm}}^{2}/{\mathrm{g}}$ for ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{MIL}}-$ 100(Fe) and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})@\mathrm{MIP}$ respectively, see Table 2. This decease can be attributed to the MIP layer growing on the surface and within the pores. Additionally, it is noted that the porous structures were not observed (around $49{\mathrm{m}}^{2}/{\mathrm{g}})$ in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-100$ (Fe)@NIP probably because the removal the template molecule CIP provide more porous structure with specific shape and size for the gas diffusion process. This result also demonstrates that the imprinted layer covers the surface and also offers porous channels around $2.3\sim10\:\mathrm{nm}$ for mass transfer (Fig. 4 (b)). This finding coincides with our previous proposed mechanism of imprinted layer graft.
Fig. 4 (c, d) shows that $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ has a lower surface area and fewer pores inside the material. After the imprint layer was grown on the surface, the surface area and pore volume did not increase significantly. It can be inferred that MIL–100 inside the core–shell structure supports the imprinted layer grow, as a result leading to a much higher surface area.
## 3.3. Removal performance
## 3.3.1. Adsorption performance
Kinetic models were applied to describe the CIP removal behavior over the as–synthesized materials. Fig. 5 (a) reveals the adsorbed kinetics up to $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ $\mathrm{{.C_{0}}=50\ p p m)}$ . During the initial stage, the uptake capacities of the three materials revealed a remarkable increase until the saturation was reached. After 2-hour reaction time, the uptake kinetics slowed down. The experimental data were fitted by two common models including pseudo secondorder kinetic model and Elovich model (Fig.S6). Results show that the Elovich model containing correlation coefficient 0.92 was suitable to fit the measured data. It is well known that the Elovich model is a useful approach to describe the activated chemisorption, indicating the strong chemical interaction existed between CIP and the adsorbents.
The distribution of CIP between liquid and solid phase was evaluated by adsorption isotherms. The CIP adsorption at room temperature (298 K) as a function of ${\sf C}_{0}$ is presented in Fig. 5 (b). Both the Langmuir and Freundlich isotherm adsorption models were applied to evaluate the adsorption performance of the material (Fig. S1 and Table S2). Based on these results, the Langmuir model $\mathrm{T}^{2}=0.996)$ is more suitable for illustrating the process of the adsorption of CIP on the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L-100(F e)@M I P}$ than the Freundlich model $\mathrm{~\boldmath~\zeta~}^{\prime}{\sf R}^{2}=0.981$ ). This result in Fig. 5(c) demonstrates that the uptake process can be successfully obtained via to the synergy process between MIL–100 and the MIP layer. This process corresponds to multilayer adsorption [39].
The maximum experimental CIP adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\$ - MIL–100(Fe)@MIP is $273.65\mathrm{mg/g}$ by Langmuir model. The adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})@\mathrm{NIP}$ to CIP is poor probably because the surface does not contain enough imprinted cavities for the adsorption process. As shown in Fig. 5 (d), ${\sf q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ value of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ - $\begin{array}{r}{0_{4}@\mathsf{M I L}-100(\mathsf{F e})@\mathsf{M I P}}\end{array}$ is $136.29\mathrm{\mg/g}$ , which is much higher than that of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}(79.22m g/g)}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MIP}$ ( $45.43\mathrm{mg/g)}$ . This result indicates that MOFs layer play a crucial role in the adsorption process.
## 3.3.2. Selective adsorption
As shown in Fig. 6 (a) and Table 3, the selectivity and adsorption capacity of MIP in this study are both observably greater than that of the existing materials reported to date, substantiating that the MOFs–based materials are among the rank of excellent CIP adsorbent. An outstanding imprinting factor ${\sf\sf{\sf{A}}}_{(\mathrm{QMIP/QNIP})}$ was achieved $(\negthickspace\negthickspace\negthickspace\negthickspace\negthickspace\negthickspace\negthickspace\negthickspace\negthickspace\textrm{Z N I P})=3.54)$ . Here ${\sf\sf{\sf{M}}}_{\mathrm{(QMIP/QNIP)}}$ representing the ratio of the adsorption capacity of imprinted and non–imprinted materials are usually used to evaluate the selectivity of imprinted materials [39]. The adsorption isotherms and kinetics of CIP were well described by using the Langmuir model and pseudo–second–order model, respectively. As mentioned above, the values of the correlation coefficient suggest that the Langmuir model is more suitable for adsorption of CIP over MIPs. Thus, the maximum adsorption capacity of CIP over $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L-100(F e)@M I P}$ was calculated by the Langmuir equation $(273.65~\mathrm{mg/g}^{\cdot}$ . Thanks to the high surface area and abounding binding sites of MOF layers inside, the maximum adsorption capacity achieved in this study. This is probably because the porous MIL–100(Fe) material has a very high specific surface area and a large number of pores compared to other base materials. These porous channels can participate in the adsorption of template molecules, thereby significantly improving the adsorption capacity and maintaining high selectivity via MIP technology.
In order to further study the selectivity, four PPCPs with different chemical structures including SPA, IBU, CBZ, and SMT were selected. As shown in Fig. 6 (b), the adsorption capacity of the four pollutants over MIP are higher than that of NIP. This is because compared with NIP materials, MIP materials have abundant pores and stronger adsorption capacity. In addition, the adsorption properties of the imprinting material and the base MOF material are compared (Fig. 6(c)). Since MIP have abundant pores and functional groups serving as the active binding sites for CIP adsorption, the recognition parameters $\upbeta$ for IBU and CBZ are 1.63 and 1.39 respectively (Table 4), indicating that the selectivity of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\cdot$ - MIL–100(Fe)@MIP to CIP is significantly enhanced after grafting the surface imprinted layer. Also, in Table 4, $\textsf{\textsf{q}}$ shows that the adsorption capacities for three pharmaceuticals decreased slightly with $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ values ranging from 0.52 to 0.85, also implying that introducing MOFs inside is one of the major reasons to reach the high capacities. Here $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ representing the ratio of the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L-100(F e)}@\mathrm{MIP}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})$ .
## 3.3.3. Application in wastewater environment and reusability
In this section, competitive adsorption has been studied in simulated wastewater (inorganic ions and NOM) and real wastewater which was collected from secondary effluents. The water parameters including inorganic ions (e.g. ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ , ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ and natural organic matters. The specific water parameters in real wastewater are listed in Section III in SI material. As we know, secondary clarifier effluents and surface waters usually contain certain amounts of natural organic matter (NOM) and trace amounts of CIP (e.g., $\mathrm{{ng-}\mu g/L)}$ [49]. NOM is ubiquitous in water and plays a predominant role in the sorption of pharmaceuticals onto sorbent particles. Each of the key parameters were evaluated separated including inorganic ions and natural organic matters. Humic acid (HA) is a common model compound of NOM and widely exists in the aquatic environment. In this section, dissolved humic acids $(200\mathrm{{mg/L})}$ and CIP solution $\mathrm{'}C_{0}=50\mathrm{mg/L}\rangle$ were added prior to initiating the adsorption process. Interestingly, it is noted that the presence of the NOM addition shows no obvious interference in CIP removal (Fig. 7 (a)). According to the previous study, the presence of humic acid usually decreases the adsorption capacity of target compounds because the presence of their carboxylic groups $(\mathsf{C O O^{-}})$ immediately influences the interaction between functional groups on adsorbent and pharmaceutical [14]. However, the presence of specific pore size with suitable shape and size of CIP can exclude other competitors in water, This structure lead to the similar adsorption capacity. On the contrary, the inorganic ions including NaCl, ${\mathsf{N a N O}}_{3}$ , ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{4}$ , KCl and $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ with high concentration $(500\ \mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) and much smaller size $(0.1\sim0.15\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) causes slight decrease $(7\%\sim58\%)$ instead. All this phenomenon demonstrates the fact that the MOF layer support the MIP grow as well as help selective removal in the aqueous environment. The evaluation of the performance in real wastewater spiked with CIP $(5\mathrm{mg}/\$ $\mathrm{L},10\mathrm{mg/L}$ and $20\mathrm{mg/L}$ ) with different concentrations are shown in Fig. 7 (b) (LC MS spectra and water parameters can be found in Fig. S2). Results show that the material can maintain the high efficiency $(>95\%$ removal in $24\ \mathrm{h}$ of CIP removal in real water with complicated constituents.
To investigate the effect of the pH value, adsorption tests were performed at various initial pH values (ranging from 3 to 11). As illustrated in Fig. 7 (c), the various pH values showed different adsorption capacities. The removal efficiency at neutral pH $(\mathsf{p H}=7^{\cdot}$ ) is the greatest. CIP has been reported as a zwitter–ionic antibiotic. The acid dissociation constants of CIP are 3.0, 5.8–6.1, 8.7–8.8 and 10.6 [50]. The adsorption of CIP over MOFs can be pH dependent. At neutral pH, the main substance is zwitterionic rather than non–ionic, cationic or anionic forms. According to the zeta potential curve of the previous material (Fig. S3), it is found that the surface of the material is positively charged at $\mathsf{p H}>4.2$ and becomes more negative as the pH value increases. CIP is mainly cationic as $\mathsf{p H}$ decreases because of protonation of the amine group $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ , while CIP exists mainly in an anionic form because of the loss of a proton from the carboxylic group $(\mathsf{C O O^{-}})$ as pH increase. As a result, acidic or alkaline pH might influence the adsorption process with the addition of different inorganic ions (e.g., ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ , ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ or $S0_{4}^{2-}$ . Thus, electrostatic attractions (due to polarization of functional groups) might influence the adsorption process. These ions may compete with CIP for the uptake on the binding site of MOF through shielding the electrostatic interaction within the pores.
On the other hand, CIP are more hydrophilic compared to zwitterionic species when $\mathsf{p H}$ increases or decreases significantly. Therefore, in the pH range of $6.0\sim8.0\$ , the hydrophobic interaction is dominated than as a result dominates the uptake process. This result is similar to the adsorption result of MIL–100(Fe) on CIP [51]. This result also demonstrates that the inner MOFs layer plays a major role in the adsorption of CIP via $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L-100(F e)@M I P}$
In order to evaluate the value of materials for practical application, experiments of reusability and recovery of a target from water were conducted. As shown in Fig. 7 (d), the adsorption performance can keep effective for a continuous adsorption–desorption for four cycles. The reagent is methanol/acetic acid (9:1) mixed solution. After washing with this reagent $\mathrm{30~mL}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ reaction time by ultrasonic method, the typical vibration peak of the
Fe-O vibration changed from $555~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $563~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ cm again. On the other hand, typical skeleton vibration peak of the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bond on the benzene ring changes from $1577~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $1573~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ after the CIP adsorption, after the material was washed by methanol and acetic acid, the vibration peak of the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bond on the benzene ring changes back to $1577~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ again. These finding proved that this regeneration method can remove the CIP adsorbed on the material surface completely (Fig.S5). This indicates that the resulting nanospheres are stable and can be repeatedly used by simple washing without significant reduction in adsorption capacity.
## 3.4. Mechanisms and degradation
To further explore the interaction between the adsorbent and CIP, XPS spectra was applied to describe the chemical elements and the corresponding chemical states. Fig. 8 (a̵d) shows the before and after adsorption of CIP. The full view XPS spectra in
Fig. 8 (a) present five characteristic peaks corresponding to Fe 2p, O 1 s, N 1S, C 1 s, Si 2 s, Si 2p respectively, indicating the presence of Fe, O, N, C and Si in the samples. Two new signals, $\mathrm{Si}_{2s}$ ( $150.5\ \mathrm{eV})$ 1 and Si 2p (100.1 eV), indicate the APTES were introduced to the surface of the layered nanoparticle [33].
Fig. 8 (b) shows that after the adsorption of CIP, the XPS spectra of Fe 2 p1/2 (725.76 eV) and Fe 2p 3/2 (712.26 eV) shifted to $725.47\ \mathrm{eV}$ and 711.97 eV respectively [37]. The XPS spectrum of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ shifted from $288.72\ \mathrm{eV}$ to $288.51\ \mathrm{eV}$ in Fig. 8 (c) indicating that the outer layer involved in the uptake process [33]. Moreover, as can be observed in Fig. 8 (d), the peak area ratio of $\mathsf{-N H}^{3+}$ increased from $31.7\%$ to $39.0\%$ . This might be due to the fact that $\boldsymbol{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ was protonated by binding additional H ions to the surface [52]. These protonated amino groups $\left(-\mathsf{N H}^{3+}\right)$ react with $\mathtt{C O O^{-}}$ groups via electrostatic interactions [53,54]. Fig. 8 (e) shows the infrared spectrum of ${\mathsf{M O F@M I P}}$ before and after CIP adsorption. After CIP is adsorbed on MIP, the stretching vibration peak of the $\mathsf{C}{\mathsf{-}}0$ bond on the carboxyl group of MIL–100(Fe) changes from $1634~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ , $1382~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ changed to $1631~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1383~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ respectively [37]. At the same time, the typical skeleton vibration peak of the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bond on the benzene ring changes from $1577~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $1573~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [40]. Furthermore, the typical framework vibration absorption peak of Fe O varies from $563~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $555~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [37]. These finding implies the MIL–100(Fe) inside the imprinted adsorbent participates in the adsorption process.
Furthermore, the magnetic properties of the material were studied (Fig. 8 (f)). The saturation magnetization values of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@.$ - MIL–100(Fe) and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})@\mathrm{MIP}$ materials are 8.16 emug1 and $3.12\mathrm{\emug}^{-1}$ respectively. The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-100$ $(\mathrm{Fe})@\mathrm{MIP}$ which is superparamagnetic can be easily collected through the magnet outside the glass bottle, which can facilitate the recovery process.
Combining the information from spectra such as IR and XPS before and after uptaking and other experimental results, the selective adsorption mechanism of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})@\mathrm{MIP}$ on CIP is proposed in Fig. 9. Furthermore, target degradation of CIP in wastewater by this molecularly imprinted MOFs has been evaluated. After CIP reached an equilibrium over $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L-100(F e}$ ) ${\ @\mathrm{MIP}}$ , $20~\mathrm{mM}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and visible–light $\mathrm{(>420~nm)}$ were added to the system subsequently. As shown in Fig. 9 (a), CIP can be completely decomposed under visible–light with added within three hours reaction time. This result implied that the imprinted layer not only provided the recognition sites for adsorption but also initiated the catalysis process in the process of visible–light Fenton degradation. It is believed that when the imprinting material $\mathsf{F e}_{3^{-}}$ $0_{4}@\mathsf{M I L}-100(\mathsf{F e})@\mathsf{M I P}$ adsorbs CIP, it is first adsorbed over the imprinted layer on the surface with specific size and shape, while other competitive constitutes in water might be excluded (Fig. 9 (b)). Then CIP diffuses into the pores in MOFs for further binding with the active sites inside. That is to say, the presence of the imprinted layer leads to the selective properties, while the presence of porous MIL–100(Fe) material with high surface area makes the imprinting material carry a high saturated adsorption capacity. Once CIP interact with MIL–100(Fe) layer with abundant reaction sites, it can be easily degraded by the photo Fenton process. The stability of the as-synthesized material was evaluated by measuring the concentration of Fe ions during the three-hour reaction time. As shown in Figure S4, the concentrations of leached Fe were detected as $0.381~\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ after $60\mathrm{{min}}$ , indicating the concentration of dissolved iron $\mathrm{(0.381~mg/L)}$ in aqueous phase was lower than the $2{\mathrm{~mg/L}},$ which is the European’s environmental standard.
## 4. Conclusions and practical implications
In conclusion, we designed magnetic surface molecularly imprinted polymers with MIL-100 as the carrier, functional monomer APTES ((3–Aminopropyl) triethoxysilane) was grafted successfully on the surface of MIL-100 for the first time. ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{MIL}}-$ ${100@\mathbf{MIP}}$ was subsequently applied in the adsorption research followed by catalytic degradation in the visible light Fenton reaction process. Combining with the detailed characterizations systematically, we found that the MOF surface shell is favorable for generating MIP layers which can facilitate the antibiotics adsorption selectively. We proposed the mechanism that MIL100 layer formed by the fast microwave irradiation method contributed to the highest adsorption amount of CIP, while imprinted layered offered the high selectivity. This material showed both ultrahigh selectivity $(\mathsf{\alpha}_{(\mathrm{QMIP/QNIP})}=3.54)_{\cdot}$ and highest uptake capacity calculated by the Langmuir equation $(273.65~\mathrm{mg/g)}$ for Ciprofloxacin so far [44,45–49]. Moreover, we found that it can maintain the high efficiency for CIP removal in simulated real water with complex compositions. The presence of the NOM addition shows no obvious interference on CIP removal, however, the inorganic ions including NaCl, ${\mathsf{N a N O}}_{3}$ , ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{4}$ KCl and $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ with high concentrations $(500\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) and much smaller sizes (0.1 $\sim0.15~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ) cause some decreases $(7\%\sim58\%)$ . Also, neutral pH can be effective in the whole process. Thus, MIL–100 (Fe) inside not only provided the recognition sites for separation but also initiated the catalysis process in the process of visible–light Fenton degradation.
Therefore, our results in this study explore the mechanism of the selective removal of CIP based on molecular imprinting. Successful catalytical degradation can be achieved for CIP removal followed by a selective uptake in wastewater. However, it is worthwhile to note that in the real wastewater, the constituents are more complicated than the simulated ones in the lab. At the same time, the micropollutants with trace-concentration among the real wastewater is challenging to be quantified. Further studies to evaluate the performance in the real wastewater is changing but also useful for next applications. On the other hand, our results only focus on MILs materials, however, the properties of MOFs including the topology and functional groups within the cavities differs a lot. Although several studies have been conducted including ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ [48,49]. The selection based on the current publications is still limited. Thus, further evaluations on the performance and mechanism should be conducted for other kinds of carries. Various studies should be conducted for finding the optimal operational parameters. Based on the aforementioned discussion, our study found that the MOFs–based nanospheres is effective in selective adsorption and have great potential for micropollutants degradation in wastewater.
